| Ticker | Sleeve | Target | Band | Role |
|--------|--------|--------|------|------|
| VWRA | Global equity (Irish UCITS Acc, LSE USD) | 60% | 50–70% | Growth |
| ITPS | US TIPS (Irish UCITS Acc; LSE USD line often IDTP) | 25% | 18–32% | Contractual real return vs US CPI |
| AGGU | Global agg USD-hedged (Irish UCITS Acc) | 15% | 9–21% | Nominal/credit diversifier |

Blended TER ~12.4 bp. All three: Irish-domiciled, accumulating, USD LSE lines. VWRA OCF confirmed 0.14% after 28 Jul 2026 cut from 0.19%.

## Rebalancing

1. Route every irregular top-up to the most underweight sleeve first
2. Wide bands as above; sell only on genuine breach, and only back to the **band edge**
3. **No calendar rebalancing**
4. Corrective sales realise USD gains — flag the tax cost; do not invent Brazilian rates

## Hard truths (do not soft-pedal)

- ITPS hedges **US CPI**, which is exactly the mandate — do not extend the claim to any other price index, and do not propose non-USD or Brazilian instruments
- In a 2022-style inflationary shock, Model A lost ~15.6%; shifting equity weight buys little protection in that scenario
- Prefer Irish UCITS Acc over US-listed cores (VT/SCHP/BNDW) for estate-tax / distribution structure — judgment call, not settled law

## Tax — mechanisms only; ask accountant

Never invent Brazilian tax rates or thresholds. Standing accountant questions:

- Accumulating Irish UCITS reporting during hold vs distributing funds
- Timing of irregular top-ups
- US estate-tax exposure if any US-situs holdings remain; does Irish UCITS eliminate vs reduce?
- Whether accumulation changes income-vs-gain character on eventual realisation

